A||||13||merlin||Heating a garage||||5||
Z||000000||merlin||01-01-2001||10:34 AM||||Ok, the cold weather has finally won out and it's just too damm cold to be working on my Fiero in a 20 degree garage.  I've tried the economical option of electric space heaters, but unless I'm sitting on top of the heater it just doesn't throw enough heat to warm my 2 car garage.  I'll probably install a permanent solution in a couple of years, but can anyone recommend a heating solution that would be under $200.  I was thinking of buying one of those kerosene heaters, but every place I look they're considered dangerous because of the potential for fire and Carbon Monoxide poisoning.  Has anyone used a kerosene heater to heat the garage and if so, how effective is it and is it that dangerous?  Is there any other low cost alternative...  ...other than wait till March?<P>Thanks.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||hugh||01-01-2001||10:49 AM||||I have a 26x30 garage when I built it I insulated the roof with 6",the walls with 4",and used insulated doors.I heat my garage with a small ceramic type heater.When It gets into the 20's or colder I set up another heater so it doesn't get too cold.The best use of your money would be to insulate if at all possible.If you don't,you are just throwing your money away.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||01-01-2001||11:00 AM||||I usually use "Mr. Heater" when working in the cold. It is a burner attachment that fits on a 20 pound propane tank. It costs about the same to use as a kerosene heater, but there is no smell or oily mess. They come in several different sizes and have different heat settings so you choose what works best for you.||||reg||1
Z||000003||2xsess||01-01-2001||11:53 AM||||I used one of those "torpedo" heaters in the garage in PA.||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||01-01-2001||12:23 PM||||I use one of those too in the garage, mine has a thermostat. They kind of look like a miniature jet engine on its side and heat up the garage a lot faster and throw more heat than the regular type kerosene heater. You should be able to get a 50,000 BTU or so unit for under $200.  Be sure to check your local ordinances, although if your garage is detached your laws may be less strict. <P>I don't use mine if I expect to generate gasoline fumes. :O<P>Some of my relatives use wood stoves in their detached garages. Those work well too except it takes longer to heat up the garage.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tina||01-01-2001||01:10 PM||||We used to use a 250 thou BTU propane construction site heater.<BR>Plus a couple of Box fans in the rafters to circulate the heat.<BR>0 to hot in less than 5 minutes, but you had to watch really carefully.<BR>First off the fumes, and then placement.<BR>Wrecked my son's bike that was stored up the raftersdirectly above, melt-down.<P>Tina<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||2xsess||01-01-2001||01:34 PM||||Mine had one of those thermostats on it too. That thing keep the garage nice and warm. Just don't aim it the wrong way  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||hugh||01-01-2001||02:28 PM||||All those high output hearters are great for heating,but you are breathing their exhaust and if you do it for long enough you may suffer for it.<BR>My 500th post,and I used it to possibly save a life(or at least prevent a headache).<p>[This message has been edited by hugh (edited 01-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||LarryB||01-01-2001||03:56 PM||||That's why you keep everything clean, use K1 kerosene and *never* use diesel, and most importantly, ventilate the room by keeping a window open an inch or so.  Huge doses of common sense are the rule.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||KRMFiero||01-01-2001||07:48 PM||||I have a 50,000 btu karosean heater i used in my garage/barn for about 5 years, this year we put some pipes under ground from our house and we put a radiator in there, keeps it warm seeing the barn is insolated and the heat is always on in the house (therefor it is always on in the barn)<P><BR>But the long karesean heaters from sears work great (look and kind of sound like a jet engine, well when turned on heheh)<P><BR>kyle||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||01-01-2001||07:53 PM||||I have a small portable electric space heater. With the walls being insulated (attached 2.5 car garage) I can get the garage up to about 65*. (takes about an hour)Insulating the walls is very very helpful. Also our house stays warmer since the garage is warmer (Under the bedrooms of the house is the garage)||||reg||1
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||01-02-2001||04:09 AM||||First step, insulate the walls.. that will make any heating solution much more effective.<P>after that, you need a heater. I personally prefer electric space heaters to propane, but it's up to you.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><P>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 01-02-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 01-02-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000012||Old Lar||01-02-2001||08:40 AM||||I used a krosene heater once.  If it isn't set up properly the fumes will kill you.  The one I used wasn't set up right. The smell and fumes drove us from the shell house we were building. We did an install of a wood stove early in the winter construction for heat. I'd go with a propane heater.||||reg||1
Z||000013||stsmithgt||01-04-2001||01:29 PM||||I prefer the propane with the ceramic heater.  With an air inlet like a window cracked open and a small fan to pull air out of the room, usually air can find it's way back into the room so the air with be replaced constantly.  The heater should be able to keep the air warm enough even with the air exchange happening. (depending on the exchange rate and tempature requirement) I would heat the room first, then start the exchange. It's clean, simple, mobile, and fairly cheap.  This ones about 60.00 bucks for 22,000 BTU's  I bought one at Harber Frieght(discount tool store) if you have something like that around. Oops picture too big... standby... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/heater1.jpg"> <P><p>[This message has been edited by stsmithgt (edited 01-04-2001).]||||reg||1
